why 1 1/4 LEADING_TONE The tune walks down the scale E♭–D–C–B♭–A♭–G, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. D is just a step on that descent.
why 2 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Alto and bass fall F–E♭ and B♭–A♭ a fifth apart, the alto an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 2 0 DOUBLING The tenor's D♭, a seventh, falls to C while the tune leaps up to C. Both land on C: a correct resolution beats the ideal doubling.
why 2 1/4 DOUBLING The soprano holds C and the tenor comes back to C after a passing B♭, while the bass drops an octave on the same A♭ chord.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (D–C) and tenor (B♭–C) meet on C by step from opposite sides.
why 4 0 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to C while the tenor steps B♭–C; both land on the third of the A♭ chord.
why 5 1/8 RANGE The tenor leaps up to A♭ to stay close under the alto's high C, then falls by step. Bach's tenors sang this high; in exercises, keep it lower.
why 5 1/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Alto and bass move A♭ to B♭ an octave apart, with the alto skipping up to C in between. For Bach the extra note separates the lines; strict exercises count it as parallel octaves.
why 5 5/8 MELODIC The bass drops a seventh from C to D instead of stepping up to D, to stay low for the coming chords. It is the step C–D, an octave lower.
why 5 5/8 OVERLAP The bass has leapt far down, so the tenor's B♭ lies below where the bass was a moment ago. With the bass now low, the lines stay clear.
why 5 3/4 SPACING The tune climbs to its high G while the alto falls to E♭. The gap lasts one beat, at the melody's peak.
why 5 3/4 CROSS_RELATION The bass's D, then D♭ in the tenor: the harmony turns at once toward A♭ (IV). The D♭ is the seventh of E♭⁷, pulling down to C, so it is heard as part of the tenor's line.
why 6 0 LEADING_TONE The tune falls G–F–E♭, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The A♭ that G leads to arrives in the bass on the same beat.
why 12 0 RANGE The bass leaps up an octave to a high E♭ and walks down E♭–D–C–B♭, keeping the chord close under the high tune.
why 12 1/8 RANGE Still high: the bass steps down from E♭ and returns to its usual range over the next beats.
why 14 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor meet on D. The tenor's D rises to E♭ as it should; the soprano's is part of the tune's descent.
why 14 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tune falls D–C here, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The E♭ that D leads to comes in the tenor and bass.
why 14 7/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT The bass skips down G–E♭–C in thirds, outlining the chords to come. Its E♭ anticipates the next harmony, reached by a skip.
why 15 3/4 SPACING The new phrase starts with the tune leaping up to its high G while the alto stays on E♭. The gap lasts a beat.
why 15 7/8 SPACING The same gap, as the tune holds its G.
why 16 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Alto and bass move from F/B♭ to G/C a fifth apart, with passing notes in both (alto G–A♭, bass B♮). Bach treats the busy lines as separate; strict exercises still count the fifths.
why 16 1/2 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass move from a unison C to an octave G in contrary motion, the tenor up and the bass down, into the fermata. Bach accepts octaves by contrary motion; strict exercises avoid them.
why 17 0 RANGE This is the hymn tune's highest note, A♭, and Bach keeps the melody where it is. In exercises, keep the soprano a little lower.
why 17 1/2 SEVENTH The bass's A♭ leaves its resolution to the alto, which takes G, while the bass leaps down to B inside the same diminished chord.
why 17 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps down a diminished seventh, A♭ to B, inside one diminished chord, then steps up to C. An expressive leap, settled by the step after it.
why 18 1/4 LEAPS After its leap up, the bass steps on up to B♭, the dominant at the fermata. It turns back one note later: the dominant is the goal.
why 19 1/2 MELODIC As in bar 5, the bass drops a seventh from C to D instead of stepping up, to stay low for the cadence. The same step, an octave lower.
why 19 1/2 OVERLAP As in bar 5, the bass has leapt far down, so the tenor's B♭ lies below where the bass just was; the lines stay clear.
why 21 0 RANGE The final chord has the bass on a deep E♭, an octave lower than usual, for a full and solemn ending.
